(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["cam-nutrition-exam-2-cam-nutrition-exam2-master-exam-form-b"]={"title":"CAM/Nutrition Exam 2 Comprehensive Master Exam — Form B","category":"CAM / Nutrition","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"What does the lecture report about evidence for the clinical benefits of Reiki?","choices":["Evidence for specific clinical benefits remains limited and inconclusive","Evidence confirms measurable transfer of energy between practitioner and patient","Evidence supports Reiki as an established first-line therapy for chronic pain","Evidence shows benefit only when the practitioner touches the skin directly"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states that Reiki is commonly used for relaxation, stress reduction, and overall well-being, but that evidence for specific clinical benefits remains limited and inconclusive. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Which conditions can be treated with chiropractic, according to the lecture?","choices":["Any condition involving pain of any origin","Infectious diseases of the spine and joints","Metabolic bone disease such as osteoporosis","Musculoskeletal conditions of the joints and surrounding soft tissue"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Examples given include neck and back pain, headache, sciatica, stiff joints, sprains and strains, herniated nucleus pulposus, and sport injuries. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which reason for the popularity of complementary and alternative medicine is listed in the lecture?","choices":["Insurance plans reimburse these therapies at higher rates than conventional care","Federal regulation guarantees the purity of every product sold","A desire for greater control over one's personal health","Clinicians receive extensive formal training in these therapies during medical education"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists a desire for greater control over personal health among the reasons for popularity, alongside the perception that these approaches are natural and safe. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which two questions may be asked to determine whether an animal is a service animal?","choices":["What the person's disability is, and who trained the dog","Whether the dog is required for a disability, and what work it does","Whether the dog is certified, and which organisation certified it","Whether the dog is licensed locally, and whether it is vaccinated"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives exactly these two questions as the only ones allowed without violating the Americans with Disabilities Act. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What correspondence does the lecture give between trigger points and classical acupuncture points?","choices":["About 95 percent","About 30 percent","About 50 percent","About 70 percent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture also links them to the origins and insertions of muscle and to motor points. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which effects does the lecture attribute more to docosahexaenoic acid than to eicosapentaenoic acid?","choices":["Lower blood pressure, better cognition, fetal brain and eye development","Reduced inflammation with mild dilation of the vessels","Reduced aggregation of the platelets only","Lowering of the low-density lipoprotein cholesterol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture attributes the mild blood pressure decrease and improved cognitive function more to docosahexaenoic acid, and fetal brain and eye development to it as well. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A homeopathic remedy called Allium cepa, made from onion, is offered for a patient with a runny nose and watery eyes. Which homeopathic theory does this illustrate?","choices":["The law of minimum dose, because the onion is diluted before use","The doctrine of biofield transfer, in which healing energy passes through the practitioner's hands","The principle that health depends on balance between yin and yang","The law of similars, because a substance that causes symptoms in healthy people is used to treat those same symptoms"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture uses this exact example: cutting onions causes burning, teary eyes and a runny nose, so Allium cepa is used for colds or hay fever under the \"like cures like\" principle. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Who does the lecture say should consult a health care provider before starting Tai Chi?","choices":["People who are pregnant or have severe osteoporosis, fractures, or a hernia","Only people already diagnosed with established heart disease","Anyone over the age of 65, regardless of fitness","Anyone who has never taken up regular exercise before"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture also names moderate to severe joint problems and back pain. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"What does the lecture say about mechanical and reflex effects?","choices":["Mechanical effects occur only in the absence of reflex effects","Mechanical effects are always accompanied by some reflex effects","Reflex effects occur only with light techniques","Reflex effects are the only effects that can be measured"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mechanical effects are direct, and reflex effects are indirect through the nervous system. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Which body scans does the lecture name?","choices":["Thermography and electromyography","Dual-energy absorptiometry and fluoroscopy","Ultrasound and bone densitometry","Positron emission tomography and single photon emission tomography"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture illustrates both a paraspinal thermographic scanner and surface electromyography. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"How does the lecture define massage therapy?","choices":["A diagnostic technique used to localise spinal dysfunction","A system based on reflex points that connect to every part of the body","An energy therapy that redirects the life force through the hands","A mechanical modality used to manipulate the body's tissue"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It promotes local and systemic relaxation, increases local blood flow, breaks down adhesions, and encourages venous return. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"On what theory is reflexology based, according to the lecture?","choices":["That energy flows along meridians named for the organs","That like cures like when sufficiently diluted","That pressure on the spine realigns the vertebrae","That reflex points on the hands, feet and head connect to the whole body"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes it as a system of massage used to relieve tension and treat illness. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Which mechanism does the lecture name alongside local and central effects?","choices":["Segmental effects and gate control","Immune suppression at the level of the spinal cord","Increased conduction velocity in motor nerves","Direct destruction of the pain fibres at the needle site"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists local effects, segmental effects and gate control, descending inhibition, and central effects. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What does a hearing or signal dog do?","choices":["Assists with navigation and environmental awareness for people who are blind","Alerts a person with hearing loss when a sound occurs","Detects the onset of psychiatric episodes and lessens their effects","Interrupts repetitive movements with an unobtrusive signal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines a hearing or signal dog as trained to alert a person with significant hearing loss or deafness when a sound occurs. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What was the focus of research into horses in the late 1800s?","choices":["The horse's role in agricultural productivity","The horse's ability to detect the onset of seizures","Breeding horses specifically for therapeutic temperament","The horse's ability to support physical recovery of humans"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes late 1800s research into the horse's ability to support physical recovery of humans. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which two regimes does the lecture identify as suppressing acupuncture?","choices":["The Tang Dynasty and the Communist Party","The Ching Dynasty and the Nationalist Regime","The Ming Dynasty and the Cultural Revolution","The Han Dynasty and the Japanese occupation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture dates the suppressions to 1644-1911 and 1911-1949 respectively. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"How is cupping performed and what is it thought to do?","choices":["A vacuum is created inside a cup placed on skin, and the resulting suction elevates skin and underlying tissue","Repeated strokes are applied with a smooth-edged instrument along the skin","Pressure is applied to specific areas of the feet, hands, or ears","Thin needles are inserted at defined points on the body in order to influence the flow of the vital force"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes creating a vacuum inside a cup placed on the skin, with suction elevating skin and underlying tissue, and notes cupping is thought to increase local blood flow. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"What tasks does the lecture give for a seizure response dog?","choices":["Barking to alert a caregiver and pressing pre-programmed devices that call for help","Detecting the seizure several hours before it begins and administering medication","Guiding the person to a hospital by a memorised route","Interpreting speech for a person who cannot hear the alarm"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists both alerting behaviour and activation of alarm or call devices. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"From what sources may homeopathic products be derived, per the lecture?","choices":["Only from flowering plants that have documented pharmacologic activity","Plants such as red onion and belladonna, minerals such as arsenic, and animals such as crushed whole bees","Only from minerals and metals that have been purified to remove organic material","Only from cultured microorganisms grown under controlled laboratory conditions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives exactly these examples of plant, mineral, and animal sources for homeopathic products. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which questions does the lecture suggest patients ask themselves about a supplement?","choices":["Rapid-improvement promises, single studies or anecdotes, and funding","Whether it is sold in a pharmacy and whether it carries a national brand","Whether it has been on the market more than five years and is widely advertised","Whether it is more expensive than similar products, implying higher purity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists exactly these questions for patients to ask themselves. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"How many meridians does the lecture describe?","choices":["Six pairs plus two unpaired, Du and Ren","Twelve pairs plus four unpaired","Five pairs, one for each element","Ten pairs plus one unpaired"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives 361 points plus extra points across them. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What skill did the medical-education equine programmes described in the lecture aim to improve?","choices":["Surgical dexterity and fine motor control skills","Diagnostic reasoning under significant time pressure","Nonverbal communication skills, and bedside manner","Pharmacology knowledge retention and recall"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture frames these programmes around improving nonverbal communication skills and bedside manner among healthcare professionals. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"How does the lecture define botanical medicine?","choices":["The use of plants or plant extracts for medicinal purposes, including algae and macroscopic fungi","The use of chelating agents that bind metals and enhance their excretion","The use of specialized therapeutic dietary patterns to prevent and to manage chronic disease","The use of vitamins and minerals to correct measured laboratory deficiencies"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines botanical medicine as the use of plants or plant extracts medicinally, consisting of plant materials, algae, macroscopic fungi, or combinations, available as solutions, powders, tablets, capsules, elixirs, topicals, or injections. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"For roughly how many conditions does the World Health Organization recommend acupuncture, according to the lecture?","choices":["Over 20 conditions","Over 50 conditions","Over 100 conditions","Over 500 conditions"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture's examples run from sinus problems and asthma to vertigo, sciatica, and morning sickness. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What are the goals of the wellness model, as the lecture states them?","choices":["Replacing conventional care with alternative therapies","Standardising treatment so every patient receives the same protocol","A patient-centred paradigm with better interdisciplinary communication","Reducing the cost of care above all other considerations"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture notes many conditions respond best to a multidisciplinary approach. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which patient groups does the lecture give as hippotherapy indications?","choices":["Patients with anxiety, post-traumatic stress disorder, and grief","Functional limitations from neuromotor and sensory dysfunction","Corporate teams seeking leadership development","Patients recovering from elective cosmetic surgery"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists functional limitations and disabilities in patients with neuromotor and sensory dysfunction, giving autism, neuromuscular disorders and cerebral palsy as examples. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of action of chelating drugs as the lecture describes it?","choices":["They stimulate the kidney to increase overall urine production","They neutralize metals chemically so the metals become inert within tissue","They bind with metals and enhance their excretion from the body","They block absorption of metals across the intestinal wall"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives this mechanism, with established uses in lead poisoning, iron overload, and other heavy metal toxicities. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"By what mechanism does St. John's Wort reduce the effectiveness of many other drugs?","choices":["It inhibits CYP3A4, raising the levels of co-administered drugs","It binds drugs in the gut and prevents their absorption","It displaces drugs from plasma protein binding sites","It induces CYP3A4, increasing the metabolism of many drugs"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture identifies St. John's Wort as a CYP3A4 inducer that increases metabolism of many drugs, including antidepressants and birth control pills. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"How does this lecture relate trigger points to acupuncture points?","choices":["Trigger points exist only in muscle, unlike acupuncture points","Trigger points can only be treated with needles","Trigger points are a counterpart of acupuncture points","Trigger points and acupuncture points are entirely unrelated"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. They may be found in muscle, tendon, myofascia, ligaments, joint capsules, periosteum and skin, and may become painful after trauma. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"What safety concern does the lecture raise specifically about Chinese herbal products?","choices":["They are effective only when combined with acupuncture in the same session","Some have been found to contain contaminants including heavy metals, pesticides, and microorganisms","They lose all activity if taken with any food or beverage","They are uniformly supported by consistent high-quality clinical evidence across every condition studied"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture reports that some Chinese herbal products have been found to contain heavy metals, pesticides, and microorganisms, and adds that herb-drug interactions should be considered when counseling patients. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"How does the lecture describe cannabidiol's effect on the psychoactivity of THC?","choices":["It intensifies THC psychoactivity by increasing CB1 affinity","It has no effect on THC psychoactivity but treats seizures independently","It appears to reduce the psychoactivity of THC by altering CB1","It blocks CB1 entirely, preventing any THC effect"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states cannabidiol appears to reduce psycho-activity and other side effects of THC by altering CB1 shape, and that taking cannabidiol with THC helps mitigate psychoactive effects. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slides 60 and 62"},{"q":"The lecture notes a labeling caution about homeopathic products. What is it?","choices":["Products labeled homeopathic must always list the original substance concentration","Products labeled homeopathic are exempt from listing any active ingredients","Products labeled as homeopathic are always diluted well beyond the point at which any active molecules could remain","Some products labeled homeopathic may not be highly diluted and can contain substantial amounts of active ingredients"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture warns that some products labeled as homeopathic may not be highly diluted and can contain substantial amounts of active ingredients, which matters for patient safety counseling. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which statement about FDA authority over dietary supplements does the lecture make?","choices":["The FDA must approve each supplement's purity before it can be sold","The FDA sets mandatory adverse-effect reporting for all supplements","The FDA reviews all supplement advertising before it may be published","It allowed good manufacturing rules, which are not rigorously followed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. DSHEA granted the FDA authority to establish GMP regulations, and the lecture notes these are not rigorously adhered to. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"In Ayurvedic theory as presented in the lecture, which dosha is associated with digestion, metabolism, and temperature?","choices":["Pitta, formed from fire and water","Vata, formed from space and air","Kapha, formed from water and earth","Qi, formed from yin and yang"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture pairs Pitta with fire and water and associates it with digestion, metabolism, and temperature. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What happened to rimonabant, and why is it relevant?","choices":["It was approved in the United States as an antiemetic and later restricted","A CB1 blocker for obesity, withdrawn in 2009 for psychiatric effects","It was a CB2 agonist withdrawn because of hepatotoxicity","It remains in use in Europe as a first-line weight loss agent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives exactly this history and notes it was never approved in the United States. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"What does the lecture report from Kleijen's meta-analysis?","choices":["That higher scientific merit made a trial less likely to favour homeopathy","That 77 percent of 107 controlled trials were positive in favour of homeopathy","That 30 percent of trials favoured homeopathy","That the analysis covered 12 trials and was inconclusive"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture adds that the higher the scientific merit, the more likely a study showed homeopathy superior to placebo. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"How long is a stationary cup left in place, according to the lecture?","choices":["About 20 to 30 minutes","About 30 seconds","About 2 to 3 minutes","About 5 to 10 minutes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Walking or sliding cups are instead moved along a meridian or across muscle fibres while still attached. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"How does the lecture describe medical cupping?","choices":["A therapy using suction to draw a blood sample for analysis","A therapy using magnets applied over selected acupuncture points","A therapy using glass cups and a flame to create a vacuum or suction on the skin","A therapy using heated smooth stones placed along the spine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture traces early written records to 28 AD, from animal horns to bamboo to glass. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"What does first-pass metabolism of delta-9 THC produce, and how does that metabolite compare?","choices":["11-hydroxy-THC, longer lasting and more psychoactive","Cannabidiol, which is non-psychotropic and anti-inflammatory","11-hydroxy-THC, which is inactive and rapidly excreted in urine","A terpene metabolite responsible for the characteristic smell"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states first-pass metabolism produces 11-hydroxy-THC, which is longer lasting and more psychoactive than delta-9 THC. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which activity does the lecture describe as a core function of the National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health?","choices":["Conducting and supporting research on the safety, effectiveness, and mechanisms of complementary health practices","Issuing and renewing the practice licenses of complementary and alternative medicine practitioners in each of the fifty states","Certifying which dietary supplements may be legally sold without a prescription","Accrediting the four-year graduate programs that train naturopathic physicians"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists exactly this function, along with providing evidence-based information for patients and healthcare professionals. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which text does the lecture name as an early written source on acupuncture?","choices":["The Canon of Medicine, written about 1000 AD","The Yellow Emperor's book of internal medicine, about 200 BC","The Hippocratic Corpus, written about 400 BC","The Compendium of Materia Medica, written about 1590 AD"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture names the Huang Di Neijing Suwen and Lingshu, dated to about 200 BC. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What was 'From Barnyard to Bedside', and who developed it?","choices":["A hippotherapy certification curriculum developed by the American Hippotherapy Association","A service dog training programme for medical students","A veterans' rehabilitation programme at Pawling Convalescent Hospital","An equine-facilitated learning programme for communication skills in medicine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes it as one of the first programmes using equine-facilitated learning to enhance communication skills and bedside manner among healthcare professionals, since reproduced at medical schools in the United States and internationally. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which statement about hypnosis does the lecture identify as a myth?","choices":["Individuals differ in how responsive they are to hypnotic suggestion","Clinical hypnotherapy uses guided relaxation and therapeutic suggestion","People lose consciousness or memory of what happened during the session","The precise mechanism underlying hypnosis remains poorly understood"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists this among the myths, clarifying that most individuals remain aware and remember the session afterward. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Complaints of false or misleading supplement advertising are handled through which agency?","choices":["The Federal Trade Commission","The Food and Drug Administration","The Drug Enforcement Administration","The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture routes complaints of false or misleading advertising, and adverse events, through the FTC. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What distinguishes animal-assisted therapy from simply spending time with an animal?","choices":["The animal must always be either a dog or a miniature horse","The animal must live at home with the patient full time","The animal must be certified by a recognised national body","The animal is used during treatment to help meet therapeutic goals"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture's definition turns on the intervention being goal-directed, with the animal used during treatment to aid achievement of therapeutic goals. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What does Jin Ye refer to?","choices":["The spirit, which governs mental clarity","The life essence inherited at birth","The blood circulating in the vessels","Body fluids such as tears and synovial fluid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives these as its examples. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is the second law of homeopathy?","choices":["That remedies must be prepared only from plant substances","That treatment must always be individualised to the patient","That curative power increases with dilution and succussion","That remedies act only in the presence of conventional treatment"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Succussion means shaking. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"What does the lecture say about pain during massage?","choices":["Pain is expected and indicates the technique is working","Pain is acceptable provided it stops within an hour","Massage should be painful only during effleurage","It should never be painful, except possibly for friction massage"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture names ecchymosis specifically as something massage should not cause. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which contaminants does the lecture warn herbal products may contain?","choices":["Only bacterial and fungal contaminating organisms","Only pesticide residues left over from cultivation","Heavy metals, unlabeled drugs, toxic plants","Only excess vitamins added during fortification"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists heavy metals, unlabeled prescription drugs and toxic plants as possible contaminants, since products are not regulated by the FDA for purity and potency. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"In the lecture's comparison table, which pairing correctly separates equine-assisted psychotherapy from equine-facilitated learning?","choices":["Psychotherapy has learning goals; facilitated learning has clinical goals","Psychotherapy involves an instructor; facilitated learning involves a therapist","Psychotherapy has clinical goals; facilitated learning has learning goals","Psychotherapy serves a participant or learner; facilitated learning serves a patient or client"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture's table contrasts mental health treatment versus educational and skill development, therapist versus instructor or facilitator, psychological disorders versus leadership and life skills, clinical versus learning goals, and patient or client versus participant or learner. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What practice experience must a hippotherapy certification applicant have?","choices":["Three years of practice, 6,000 hours, and 100 hippotherapy hours","At least one year of practice and 20 hours of hippotherapy","At least five years of practice and 500 hours of hippotherapy","No practice requirement, only a written examination"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists three years or 6,000 hours in practice, at least 100 hours of hippotherapy practice within the previous 3 years, and a passed multiple-choice examination. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Are emotional support and comfort animals service animals under Titles II and III of the Americans with Disabilities Act?","choices":["Yes, if the animal is a dog","Yes, provided a licensed professional has written a letter","No, they are expressly not service animals under those titles","Only in healthcare settings"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states emotional support animals and comfort animals are not service animals under Title II and Title III of the Americans with Disabilities Act, and do not have the same protection. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"If swelling is present in an extremity, where should treatment begin?","choices":["Distally, working gradually towards the trunk","At the point where the swelling is greatest","Proximally, in what the lecture calls uncorking the bottle","Only once the swelling has fully resolved"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture names the uncorking effect explicitly. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which organs does the lecture assign to the water element?","choices":["Lung and large intestine","Gallbladder and liver","Urinary bladder and kidney","Stomach and spleen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture pairs water with the urinary bladder and the kidney. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What does the lecture say flaxseed does to the absorption of other medications?","choices":["It can slow the absorption of all medications","It speeds the absorption of all medications","It has no effect on the absorption of other medications","It blocks absorption of fat-soluble vitamins only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states flaxseed can slow the absorption of all medications, and lists interactions with anticoagulants, diabetes medications, contraceptives, hormones and antihyperlipidaemic agents. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"How does the lecture define a myofascial trigger point?","choices":["An area of skin showing reduced resistance to electrical current on testing","A small area of 3 to 5 millimetres within a muscle that remains abnormal after an injury","A palpable thickening of the tendon where it inserts onto the bone","A point of maximal tenderness lying directly over a named peripheral nerve"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It may cause chronic pain and stiffness and can propagate to fibrositis or fibromyalgia. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Why does the lecture say a medium is used in massage?","choices":["To warm the tissue before deep work","To deliver medication through the skin","To sterilise the skin before treatment","To decrease friction between the patient's skin and your hand"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Massage can also be given without any medium, and more is needed on hairy areas. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"What general dosing principle does the lecture give for medical marijuana?","choices":["Load with a high dose, then reduce once symptoms settle","Follow the principle of low and slow","Dose by body weight, as with paediatric herbal dosing","Give a fixed 60 mg daily dose regardless of route"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states the general principle is low and slow, and notes that over 10 mg of THC at one time can produce euphoria. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which caution does the lecture attach to milk thistle?","choices":["It may raise blood pressure, so avoid it with antihypertensives","It may cause allergic skin reactions when used topically","It may cause photosensitivity, so patients should limit sun exposure","It may lower blood sugar, so use caution in diabetes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture warns that milk thistle may lower blood sugar and that people with diabetes or hypoglycemia, or those on drugs affecting blood sugar, should use caution. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What do studies suggest equine-assisted psychotherapy may improve in traumatised youth?","choices":["Gross motor coordination and balance","Trust and secure attachment","Academic test performance","Medication adherence"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture notes studies suggest equine-assisted psychotherapy may improve trust and secure attachment in traumatised youth. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 33"}]};
